Vulnerability Title

Authorization Bypass via Response Manipulation Allowing Access to Restricted Modules

Risk Level

High

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

8.2 – High

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N

Vulnerability Category

Broken Access Control / Authorization Bypass

OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A01: Broken Access Control
https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

Vulnerability Description

The application implements role-based access control to restrict user access to certain modules such as Post Trade Compliance, Pre Allocation, and other restricted modules. During testing, it was observed that users without appropriate permissions are normally denied access to these modules.

However, by intercepting the server response using a proxy tool and modifying a specific authorization parameter in the response from false to true, the application allowed the user to access the restricted modules.

This indicates that the application relies on client-side authorization checks instead of enforcing proper server-side access control validation, making it possible for attackers to manipulate responses and gain unauthorized access to restricted functionalities.

Impact

Unauthorized users can access restricted modules within the application.

Exposure of sensitive financial and portfolio management functionalities.

Attackers may manipulate or view confidential business data.

Potential compromise of business logic and workflow integrity.

Could lead to unauthorized financial decisions or operational misuse within the platform.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Login to the application using a user account that does not have access to restricted modules such as Post Trade Compliance or Pre Allocation.

Step 2: Navigate to a restricted module or initiate a request that checks access permissions.

Step 3: Intercept the server response using Burp Suite Proxy.

Step 4: Identify the authorization flag or parameter in the response (e.g., access:false).

Step 5: Modify the value from false to true.

Step 6: Forward the modified response to the browser.

Step 7: Observe that the restricted module becomes accessible despite the user not having proper authorization.

Recommendations

Implement strict server-side authorization checks for all restricted modules.

Do not rely on client-side responses or flags to determine access control decisions.

Validate user permissions on every request at the server level.

Implement role-based access control (RBAC) with proper backend enforcement.

Monitor and log unauthorized access attempts for security auditing.

Perform regular security testing and code reviews to detect access control weaknesses.

